+# Spec: overdue fines and member accounts
+
+(The holds spec follows this one.)
+
+## Why
+
+Late returns currently cost nothing, so DVDs come back weeks late. Members get
+an account: a late return charges a fine to it, staff can add manual charges
+(lost card, damaged case), members can pay, and a member who owes too much
+can't borrow until they pay some of it off.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+All on `LendingService` in `src/lending/service.py`:
+
+- `return_copy(copy_id)` keeps its signature and return value; a late return now also charges the fine.
+- `balance(member_id: str) -> Decimal` — what the member owes right now.
+- `add_charge(member_id: str, amount: Decimal, reason: str) -> Decimal` — manual staff charge; returns the new balance.
+- `pay(member_id: str, amount: Decimal) -> Decimal` — records a payment; returns the new balance.
+
+Add to `src/lending/errors.py`:
+
+- `AccountBlocked(LendingError)`, raised by `checkout` when the member owes too much.
+- `InvalidAmount(LendingError)` for amounts that can't be accepted.
+
+Unknown members keep raising `MemberNotFound`.
+
+## Rules
+
+### Fines
+
+- Rates per late day: **book 0.25**, **DVD 1.00**. No currency is modelled; amounts are `Decimal`.
+- **Late days** are the days after `due_on`, up to and including the return day,
+  that the library is open (Sundays don't count).
+- A copy returned on or before its due date costs nothing.
+- One loan's fine is capped at the title's `replacement_cost`.
+- **Children are never fined.** Their late returns go through as normal and no charge is posted.
+- The fine is posted to the balance when the copy is returned. Each late return
+  posts its own fine.
+
+### Balance
+
+- The balance is the sum of posted charges (fines and manual charges) minus
+  payments. Fines still building up on overdue copies that haven't come back
+  are **not** included.
+- The balance is never negative. The library doesn't hold credit.
+
+### Blocking
+
+- `checkout` raises `AccountBlocked` when the member's balance is **10.00 or
+  more**. Exactly 10.00 blocks. The threshold is the same for every member category.
+- A blocked member can still return copies and pay.
+- Check order in `checkout`: unknown member (`MemberNotFound`) → unknown copy
+  (`CopyNotFound`) → `AccountBlocked` → `CopyNotAvailable` → `LoanLimitReached`.
+
+### Amounts
+
+- A charge or payment amount must be positive and in whole cents. Otherwise
+  `InvalidAmount` is raised and nothing changes.
+- A payment larger than the current balance is rejected with `InvalidAmount`.
+- `reason` is stored as given. It isn't validated.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. A new member's `balance` is 0.
+2. `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay` raise `MemberNotFound` for an unknown member.
+3. Returning a copy on or before its due date leaves the balance unchanged.
+4. A DVD due Mon 9 Mar 2026 and returned Mon 16 Mar 2026 is 6 late days
+   (Sun 15 Mar doesn't count) and charges 6.00.
+5. A book returned 1 open day late charges 0.25. A book returned 4 open days late charges 1.00.
+6. A return the day after a Sunday counts that Sunday as no late day: a loan due
+   Sat and returned the following Mon is 1 late day.
+7. A loan's fine never exceeds the title's replacement cost (e.g. a DVD with
+   replacement cost 12.00 returned 20 open days late charges 12.00).
+8. A child returning late is charged nothing. The loan still closes and the copy
+   goes back on the shelf.
+9. `return_copy` still returns the closed `Loan`, with `returned_on` set, for late returns as well.
+10. Two late returns by the same member each add their own fine to the balance.
+11. An overdue copy that hasn't been returned adds nothing to the balance.
+12. `add_charge` raises the balance by the amount and returns the new balance.
+13. `pay` lowers the balance by the amount and returns the new balance. Paying
+    exactly the balance leaves 0.
+14. `pay` with an amount above the balance raises `InvalidAmount`, and the balance is unchanged.
+15. `add_charge` and `pay` raise `InvalidAmount` for zero, negative, or
+    fractional-cent amounts (e.g. 0.001), and the balance is unchanged.
+16. `checkout` raises `AccountBlocked` when the balance is 10.00. It succeeds at 9.99.
+17. A blocked member can return a copy and can pay. After paying below 10.00 they can borrow again.
+18. A blocked member asking for a copy that's already on loan gets
+    `AccountBlocked`. An unknown member gets `MemberNotFound`. An unknown copy
+    gets `CopyNotFound`.
+19. `AccountBlocked` and `InvalidAmount` are subclasses of `LendingError`.
+20. The overdue report's output is unchanged.
+21. Every test that existed before this change still passes.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Viewing an account's transaction history (charges and payments are still kept
+  with their date and reason, so this can be added later).
+- Waiving or refunding fines. Credit balances.
+- Lost-item charges as a feature (staff use manual charges).
+- Changes to the overdue report.
+- Fines building up on items still out, for either the balance or blocking.
+- Holds and no-show fees (now specified in "Spec: holds" below). Renewals (see
+  "Future direction" at the end).
+
+---
+
+# Spec: holds
+
+## Why
+
+When every copy of a title is out, members can only keep asking at the desk.
+With holds, a member joins a queue for the title. A returned copy is set aside
+for the first person in line, who gets a week to pick it up. A daily job clears
+holds nobody collected, charges the no-show fee, and passes the copy to the
+next person.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+On `LendingService` in `src/lending/service.py`:
+
+- `place_hold(member_id: str, isbn: str) -> Hold`
+- `cancel_hold(hold_id: str) -> Hold`
+- `expire_holds() -> list[Hold]`: the daily job. Returns the holds it expired.
+- `holds_for(isbn: str) -> list[Hold]`: active holds on the title, first in line first.
+- `return_copy` and `checkout` keep their signatures.
+
+In `src/lending/models.py`:
+
+- dataclass `Hold(hold_id: str, member_id: str, isbn: str, placed_on: date, status: HoldStatus, copy_id: str | None, pickup_by: date | None)`
+- enum `HoldStatus` with `WAITING, READY, FULFILLED, CANCELLED, EXPIRED`
+- new member `CopyStatus.ON_HOLD_SHELF`
+
+In `src/lending/errors.py`:
+
+- `HoldNotAllowed(LendingError)`
+- `HoldNotFound(LendingError)`
+
+Existing error classes are still used where they fit: `MemberNotFound`,
+`TitleNotFound`, `AccountBlocked`, `CopyNotAvailable`, `LoanLimitReached`.
+
+## Rules
+
+### Hold states
+
+- **WAITING**: in the queue, no copy yet.
+- **READY**: a copy (`copy_id`) is set aside on the hold shelf until `pickup_by`.
+- **FULFILLED**: the member borrowed a copy of the title.
+- **CANCELLED**: cancelled before it was fulfilled.
+- **EXPIRED**: was READY and not collected by `pickup_by`.
+- WAITING and READY are *active*. The other three are final.
+- `copy_id` and `pickup_by` are `None` while a hold is WAITING.
+
+### Placing a hold
+
+Checks run in this order. The first one that fails raises its error, and nothing changes:
+
+1. Unknown member: `MemberNotFound`.
+2. Unknown ISBN: `TitleNotFound`.
+3. Member owes **5.00 or more**: `AccountBlocked`. This bar is deliberately lower
+   than the 10.00 borrowing bar. It's the same for every member category.
+4. `HoldNotAllowed` if any of these is true:
+   - a copy of the title is `AVAILABLE` (the member should just borrow it);
+   - the title has no copies in circulation (none, or all withdrawn);
+   - the member already has an active hold on the title;
+   - the member currently has a copy of the title on loan.
+
+A new hold is WAITING, `placed_on` is today, and it goes to the back of the
+queue. Children can place holds. There's no limit on the number of holds per member.
+
+### Queue order
+
+- The queue for a title is its active holds in the order they were placed.
+  Holds placed on the same day keep that order.
+- `holds_for(isbn)` returns exactly that list. An unknown ISBN raises `TitleNotFound`.
+
+### Setting a copy aside
+
+- When a copy becomes free (returned, newly added, or released by a cancelled,
+  expired or bypassed READY hold) and the title has a WAITING hold, the copy goes
+  to the **first WAITING hold** in the queue:
+  - the copy becomes `ON_HOLD_SHELF`;
+  - the hold becomes READY, with `copy_id` set to that copy and `pickup_by` as below.
+- When nobody is waiting, the copy goes back to `AVAILABLE` (a newly added copy
+  just stays `AVAILABLE`).
+- **`pickup_by`** = the day the copy is set aside + **7 days**. If that falls on
+  a Sunday, it's the following Monday. The same rule applies whether the copy was
+  set aside by a return, a new copy, a cancellation, a bypassed hold, or the expiry job.
+- `return_copy` still returns the closed `Loan`. Late fines are charged exactly as before.
+
+### Picking up
+
+- The member collects through `checkout(member_id, copy_id)`.
+- A copy that's `ON_HOLD_SHELF` can be borrowed only by the member whose READY
+  hold it's set aside for. Anyone else gets `CopyNotAvailable`.
+- The normal checkout rules still apply at pickup, in the existing order:
+  `MemberNotFound` → `CopyNotFound` → `AccountBlocked` (10.00) →
+  `CopyNotAvailable` → `LoanLimitReached`. If pickup fails, the hold stays READY,
+  with its copy still set aside until `pickup_by`.
+- The member can collect on any day up to and including `pickup_by`, as long as
+  the expiry job hasn't expired the hold.
+- When a member with an active hold on a title borrows **any** copy of that
+  title, the hold becomes FULFILLED. If that hold was READY with a different
+  copy set aside, that copy is freed and passed on (see "Setting a copy aside").
+
+### Cancelling
+
+- `cancel_hold` on a WAITING or READY hold makes it CANCELLED. No fee is charged.
+- Cancelling a READY hold frees its copy, which is passed on with today + 7
+  (Sunday rule) as the next person's `pickup_by`, or goes back to `AVAILABLE`.
+- Unknown `hold_id`: `HoldNotFound`. A hold that's already FULFILLED, CANCELLED
+  or EXPIRED: `HoldNotAllowed`, and nothing changes.
+
+### Expiring (daily job)
+
+- `expire_holds()` expires every READY hold whose `pickup_by` is **before today**.
+  A hold whose `pickup_by` is today is left alone.
+- For each expired hold:
+  - the hold becomes EXPIRED;
+  - an adult is charged a **1.00 no-show fee**, posted to the same balance as fines.
+    It counts toward both the 10.00 borrowing block and the 5.00 hold block.
+    Children are never charged it;
+  - the copy is passed on with today + 7 (Sunday rule) as the next person's
+    `pickup_by`, or goes back to `AVAILABLE`.
+- It returns the holds it expired, in placement order. With nothing to expire it returns `[]`.
+- It catches up if runs were missed. Running it again on the same day expires
+  nothing new and charges nothing. A copy passed on today isn't expired by the same run.
+- WAITING holds never expire.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `Hold`, `HoldStatus` (with exactly `WAITING, READY, FULFILLED, CANCELLED, EXPIRED`),
+   `CopyStatus.ON_HOLD_SHELF`, `HoldNotAllowed` and `HoldNotFound` exist as named in
+   the interface. Both errors subclass `LendingError`.
+2. With every copy of a title on loan, `place_hold` returns a WAITING hold for that
+   member and ISBN, with `placed_on` today and `copy_id` and `pickup_by` both `None`.
+3. `place_hold` raises `MemberNotFound` for an unknown member and `TitleNotFound` for an unknown ISBN.
+4. `place_hold` raises `AccountBlocked` for a member owing 5.00, and succeeds at 4.99.
+   A member owing 5.00 who also tries to hold a title that has an available copy gets `AccountBlocked`.
+5. `place_hold` raises `HoldNotAllowed` when a copy of the title is available, when
+   the title has no copies or only withdrawn ones, when the member already has an
+   active (WAITING or READY) hold on it, and when the member has a copy of it on loan.
+6. A child can place a hold.
+7. `holds_for` lists active holds in placement order and leaves out FULFILLED,
+   CANCELLED and EXPIRED holds. It raises `TitleNotFound` for an unknown ISBN.
+8. Returning a copy whose title has WAITING holds: the copy becomes `ON_HOLD_SHELF`,
+   only the first hold becomes READY with that `copy_id`, and `return_copy` still
+   returns the closed loan. A late return still charges its fine.
+9. `pickup_by` is 7 days after the set-aside day: set aside Mon 2 Mar 2026 → Mon
+   9 Mar 2026. When day + 7 is a Sunday, it's the Monday after: set aside Sun 1 Mar
+   2026 → Mon 9 Mar 2026.
+10. Returning a copy with nobody waiting puts it back to `AVAILABLE`, as before.
+11. The member a copy is set aside for can borrow it with `checkout`. The hold becomes
+    FULFILLED and leaves `holds_for`, and the copy is `ON_LOAN`. Another member trying
+    to borrow it gets `CopyNotAvailable`.
+12. At pickup, a member owing 10.00 gets `AccountBlocked`, and a member at the loan limit
+    gets `LoanLimitReached`. In both cases the hold stays READY and the copy stays `ON_HOLD_SHELF`.
+13. A member whose hold is READY for one copy who borrows a different (available) copy
+    of the same title gets the hold FULFILLED, and the set-aside copy goes back to
+    `AVAILABLE`. (A copy is only ever on the shelf when nobody is waiting, so in
+    practice there's no next hold to pass it to.) A member with a READY hold can't
+    borrow a copy set aside for someone else.
+14. Adding a copy to a title with WAITING holds sets it aside for the first one.
+15. `cancel_hold` on a WAITING hold makes it CANCELLED and removes it from the queue.
+    The rest keep their order.
+16. `cancel_hold` on a READY hold makes it CANCELLED and passes the copy to the next
+    WAITING hold with `pickup_by` = today + 7. With nobody waiting, the copy becomes `AVAILABLE`.
+    No fee is charged.
+17. `cancel_hold` raises `HoldNotFound` for an unknown id, and `HoldNotAllowed` for a
+    FULFILLED, CANCELLED or EXPIRED hold.
+18. `expire_holds` on the `pickup_by` day expires nothing. The day after, it expires the
+    hold, returns it, charges an adult 1.00, and passes the copy on with today + 7 (or
+    shelves it).
+19. A child's expired hold charges nothing.
+20. Running `expire_holds` twice on the same day: the second run returns `[]` and charges nothing.
+21. If the job is first run several days late, it still expires every overdue READY hold.
+    It doesn't expire a hold whose copy was passed on during that run.
+22. WAITING holds are never expired.
+23. No-show fees count toward blocks: an adult owing 4.00 who is charged a no-show fee
+    can no longer place holds.
+24. The overdue report's output is unchanged. Every test that existed before this change still passes.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Notifying members (email, SMS, letters) that a copy is ready.
+- A limit on the number of holds per member.
+- Expiring WAITING holds, or a "not needed after" date.
+- Holds on a particular copy, or choosing a pickup branch.
+- Moving members up or down the queue, or staff overrides.
+- Fees for cancelling.
+- Renewals (below).
+
+## Future direction (not built now; this change must not get in their way)
+
+- **Renewals** (now specified in "Spec: renewals" below):
+  - Renewing an overdue loan posts the late fine accrued so far, counted exactly as
+    for a return on the renewal day (same rates, cap and child exemption). After the
+    renewal, lateness is counted against the new due date. Lateness before a renewal
+    must never just disappear. So the fine calculation must work for any "as if
+    returned on" day, not only at return time.
+  - While anyone is still **WAITING** in the queue for a title, loans of that title
+    can't be renewed. A READY hold doesn't count, because that person already has
+    their copy.
+  - Only paid-up members can renew. If the member's balance shows anything at all
+    owed when they ask, they can't renew until they have paid. This is a third
+    block threshold, after 10.00 to borrow and 5.00 to hold.
+
+---
+
+# Spec: renewals
+
+## Why
+
+Members ask to keep books longer. Staff currently do this by returning the
+copy and checking it out again, which splits one borrowing into two loans and
+breaks the loan history. A renewal extends the open loan in place, so the
+history shows one loan, and it records how many times that loan was renewed.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan` in `src/lending/service.py` — renews the open loan in
+  place (same `loan_id`) and returns it.
+- `Loan` in `src/lending/models.py` gets a field `renewals: int` (default `0`): how many times the
+  loan has been renewed.
+- Add `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` to `src/lending/errors.py` for renewals that can't be granted;
+  unknown loan ids keep raising `LoanNotFound`, and keep using the other existing error classes where
+  they already fit.
+
+`checkout`, `return_copy` and every other existing method keep their signatures
+and return values.
+
+## Rules
+
+### What a renewal does
+
+- A renewal is not a new loan. The loan keeps its `loan_id`, `copy_id`,
+  `member_id` and `borrowed_on`. The copy stays `ON_LOAN`. No new loan appears
+  in `loan_history`.
+- The new `due_on` is counted from the **renewal day**, exactly like a checkout
+  made that day: renewal day + 21 days, moved to the Monday if it lands on a
+  Sunday. Example: a book due Mon 23 Mar 2026 renewed Thu 19 Mar 2026 is due
+  Thu 9 Apr 2026.
+- `renewals` goes up by 1. `returned_on` stays `None`.
+
+### Who and what can be renewed
+
+Checks run in this order. The first one that fails raises its error, and
+nothing changes (due date, renewal count and balance all stay as they were):
+
+1. Unknown `loan_id`: `LoanNotFound`.
+2. The loan is already returned: `RenewalRefused`.
+3. The copy is a **DVD**: `RenewalRefused`. DVDs are never renewable. Only books are.
+4. Too early: a loan can be renewed from **5 days before its due date**
+   onwards. Due Mon 23 Mar 2026 → renewable from Wed 18 Mar 2026. On Tue 17 Mar
+   it's `RenewalRefused`. There's no latest day, so overdue loans can be renewed.
+5. The member owes **anything at all** (balance above 0.00): `AccountBlocked`.
+   This is the third block threshold, after 10.00 to borrow and 5.00 to hold.
+   It's the same for every member category. The balance is checked before
+   any renewal fine is posted (see below).
+6. Anyone has a **WAITING** hold on the loan's title: `RenewalRefused`. READY
+   holds don't count, because that person already has a copy set aside.
+7. The loan has already been renewed **2 times**: `RenewalRefused`. The limit is
+   the same for adults and children.
+
+### Renewing an overdue loan
+
+- Renewing a loan after its due date first posts the fine accrued so far,
+  counted exactly like a return on the renewal day: same rates, Sundays
+  skipped, child exemption. The fine is posted to the member's balance like a
+  return fine. Then the loan is renewed as usual.
+- After a renewal, lateness is counted only against the new due date. Lateness
+  before a renewal is always fined once, never forgotten and never charged twice.
+- The replacement-cost cap applies to the **whole loan**. The fines posted for
+  one loan (at renewals and at the final return) together never exceed the
+  title's `replacement_cost`.
+- A member with a 0.00 balance can renew an overdue loan. The fine is posted
+  and the renewal is granted, but they must pay it before they can renew again.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `RenewalRefused` exists in `lending.errors` and subclasses `LendingError`.
+   A new loan's `renewals` is 0.
+2. `renew` returns the same loan (same `loan_id`, `copy_id`, `member_id`,
+   `borrowed_on`), still open, with `renewals` 1. `get_loan` shows the change.
+   The copy stays `ON_LOAN`. `loan_history` still holds exactly one loan for it.
+3. A book due Mon 23 Mar 2026 renewed on Thu 19 Mar 2026 is due Thu 9 Apr 2026.
+4. A renewal on a Sunday whose +21 days is a Sunday gets a Monday due date
+   (renewed Sun 22 Mar 2026 → due Mon 13 Apr 2026).
+5. A book due Mon 23 Mar 2026 can be renewed on Wed 18 Mar 2026 and on its due
+   date. On Tue 17 Mar 2026 `renew` raises `RenewalRefused`.
+6. `renew` raises `LoanNotFound` for an unknown loan id.
+7. `renew` raises `RenewalRefused` for a returned loan.
+8. `renew` raises `RenewalRefused` for a DVD loan, even inside the window.
+9. A loan can be renewed twice. The third `renew` raises `RenewalRefused`, and
+   the loan keeps `renewals` 2 and its due date. The same holds for a child.
+10. `renew` raises `AccountBlocked` when the member owes anything (e.g. 0.01),
+    for adults and children. After paying the balance to 0.00 the renewal succeeds.
+11. `renew` raises `RenewalRefused` while another member has a WAITING hold on
+    the title. A READY hold on the title (another copy set aside) doesn't stop it.
+12. A refused renewal changes nothing: due date, `renewals` and balance are
+    unchanged.
+13. Renewing a book 3 open days overdue posts a 0.75 fine and renews it. Its
+    new due date is the renewal day + 21.
+14. A later late return fines only the open days after the new due date. The
+    days fined at renewal are not fined again.
+15. A child renewing an overdue loan is charged nothing, and the renewal is granted.
+16. The fines posted for one loan, at renewal and at return, never add up to
+    more than the title's replacement cost.
+17. After an overdue renewal posts a fine, a second `renew` raises
+    `AccountBlocked` until the fine is paid.
+18. A renewed loan isn't overdue (and isn't in the overdue report) until after
+    its new due date.
+19. Every test that existed before this change still passes.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- A history of individual renewals (dates, who renewed). Only the count is kept.
+- Staff overrides of the limit, the window, or the blocks.
+- Automatic or self-service renewal, and notifications.
+- Renewing DVDs.
+- Changes to the overdue report beyond it using the loan's current `due_on`.
